      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "bcb45a78-bb0a-5acf-b8b7-22b1ec3dd669",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Chamelaucium, Vanilla, Verticordia habrantha\n- Taxonomy: Myrtaceae, Leptospermum, orchids, V. planifolia\n- Places: south western Western Australia, Mexico\n- Products: vanilla flavoring\n\nFacts:\n- Chamelaucium, also known as waxflower, is a genus of shrubs endemic to south western Western Australia.\n- Chamelaucium belongs to the myrtle family Myrtaceae and has flowers similar to those of Leptospermum tea-trees.\n- Vanilla is a flavoring derived from orchids of the genus Vanilla, primarily from the Mexican species V. planifolia.\n- Verticordia habrantha has hairy sepals mostly hidden by round, unfringed petals, causing it to resemble shrubs in the genus Chamelaucium.\n- Verticordia habrantha is closely related to Chamelaucium.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "218826f5-4727-50ec-8805-0b4ad5261c51",
        "text": "Page: Chamelaucium. Sentence: Chamelaucium, also known as waxflower, is a genus of shrubs endemic to south western Western Australia.\nPage: Chamelaucium. Sentence: They belong to the myrtle family Myrtaceae and have flowers similar to those of the tea-trees (\"Leptospermum\").\nPage: Vanilla. Sentence: Vanilla is a flavoring derived from orchids of the genus \"Vanilla\", primarily from the Mexican species, flat-leaved vanilla (\"V. planifolia\").\nPage: Verticordia habrantha. Sentence: Its hairy sepals are mostly hidden by the round, unfringed petals, and as a result, the plant looks like shrubs in the genus \"Chamelaucium\", to which it is closely related.",
